WEBVTT

00:00:11.890 --> 00:00:24.850
<v Ira Glass>OK, so this week, like the rest of us, Ohio is going to the polls. And in Ohio, there are more people who vote Republican there, but not a lot more. Donald Trump and Barack Obama each carried the state twice.
<v Ira Glass>好的，这周和我们大家一样，俄亥俄州也要进行投票。在俄亥俄州，投共和党票的人数确实比投民主党的人数多，但差距并不大。唐纳德·特朗普和巴拉克·奥巴马都曾两次赢下该州。

00:00:24.850 --> 00:00:53.260
<v Ira Glass>If the Congressional delegation reflected the breakdown of Democratic and Republican voters in the state, they would have eight Republicans and seven Democrats sitting in the House of Representatives. But right now, it is not that. There are not seven Democrats. There are four. And that's probably the best outcome the Democrats can hope for in this week's election. In fact, they could do worse, which, of course, would hurt the Democrats' chances for holding control of the US House of Representatives.
<v Ira Glass>如果国会代表团能够反映该州民主党和共和党选民的构成比例，那么众议院里应该有八名共和党人和七名民主党人。但现在的情况并非如此。民主党人不是七名，而是四名。而这可能是民主党在本周选举中能期待的最好结果了。事实上，他们的情况可能会更糟，这当然会损害民主党维持对美国众议院控制权的机会。

00:00:53.260 --> 00:01:09.480
<v Ira Glass>And why, you may ask, are Democrats only going to take two or three or four seats and not seven? At one level, it doesn't have anything to do with the issues or politics or Biden or anything like that. It really just comes down to gerrymandering.
<v Ira Glass>你可能会问，为什么民主党只能拿到两三个或四个席位，而不是七个？从某种程度上讲，这与议题、政治倾向、拜登或其他类似因素毫无关系。归根结底，这只是选区划分不公的问题。

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:26.270
<v Ira Glass>The districts are drawn to favor the Republicans, mostly by smashing lots of Democrats into just a few districts, giving Republicans majorities in the rest of them. In Ohio, those districts are about as bad as they come. But here's what's different about Ohio.
<v Ira Glass>选区划分倾向于共和党，主要是通过将大量民主党选民挤入少数几个选区，从而使共和党在其余选区获得多数优势。在俄亥俄州，这些选区的划分简直糟糕透顶。但俄亥俄州的情况有所不同。

00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:49.730
<v Ira Glass>A few years back, something very unusual happened. Republicans and Democrats came together, and they said, enough. They changed the state's constitution so the districts would be drawn fairly at last. 70% of voters in 2015 and 2018 approved this. Arnold Schwarzenegger-- he's fought against gerrymandering going back to when he was governor of California. He tweeted a congratulatory video.
<v Ira Glass>几年前，发生了一件非常不同寻常的事。共和党人和民主党人走到了一起，他们说：受够了。他们修改了州宪法，以便最终能公正地划分选区。2015年和2018年，70%的选民批准了这一举措。阿诺德·施瓦辛格——从他担任加州州长时起，他就一直在反对选区划分不公。他发推特发布了一段祝贺视频。

00:01:49.730 --> 00:02:01.400
<v Arnold Schwarzenegger>And I also want to congratulate the legislators, Democrats and Republicans alike, for coming together, and all of you together have terminated gerrymandering in Ohio. That's fantastic.
<v Arnold Schwarzenegger>我也要祝贺议员们，无论是民主党人还是共和党人，感谢你们齐心协力，你们所有人共同终结了俄亥俄州的选区划分不公。这太棒了。

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:03.650
<v Ira Glass>Hard not to love how on-brand that guy is.
<v Ira Glass>很难不爱这个家伙如此鲜明的个人风格。

00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:08.660
<v Arnold Schwarzenegger>That's a huge victory, getting rid of gerrymandering, which is the worst thing in America.
<v Arnold Schwarzenegger>这是一个巨大的胜利，摆脱了选区划分不公，这是美国最糟糕的事情。

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:18.770
<v Ira Glass>It was a rare kumbaya political moment. The Republican governor of Ohio, John Kasich, was praising Democrats and voters rights groups, making idealistic speeches.
<v Ira Glass>那是一个难得的政治和解时刻。俄亥俄州共和党州长约翰·卡西奇称赞了民主党人和选民权利组织，并发表了理想主义的演讲。

00:02:18.770 --> 00:02:43.350
<v John Kasich>Listen, this is incredible, what they did. We're now a model for the country, and it just means we're going to have fair elections. They're going to be more competitive. Why does this issue matter so much? Well, because when you draw lines in such a way that only one party can win the district, it creates a polarizing effect.
<v John Kasich>听着，他们所做的一切简直不可思议。我们现在成为了全国的榜样，这意味着我们将举行公平的选举。选举将会更具竞争性。为什么这个问题如此重要？嗯，因为当你以这种方式划分选区，使得只有一个政党能赢时，它会产生一种两极分化的效应。

00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:55.050
<v Ira Glass>That's because, he says, when candidates don't have to worry about voters from the other party voting them out, their views get more extreme. They play to their base, court voters from their own party with whatever red meat issues are popular.
<v Ira Glass>这是因为，他说，当候选人不必担心另一党的选民会投票将他们赶下台时，他们的观点就会变得更加极端。他们迎合自己的基本盘，用任何受欢迎的激进议题来拉拢本党选民。

00:02:55.050 --> 00:03:22.290
<v John Kasich>And that means we move farther and farther apart as a nation. And it all leads to-- [COUGHS] excuse me-- getting nothing done. This is really big time. This is the beginning of an effort to end all this polarization, get people to communicate, to listen to one another. That's what this is about.
<v John Kasich>这意味着我们作为一个国家会渐行渐远。这一切最终导致——（咳嗽声）不好意思——什么事也做不成。这真的是一件大事。这是结束所有这种两极分化、让人们沟通并倾听彼此努力的开始。这就是这件事的意义所在。

00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:46.310
<v Ira Glass>And so the new rules of map making went into effect. Ohio headed into the utopian post-gerrymandering world where people would communicate and polarization would shrink, at least a little. And in 2021, they got their very first chance to draw new completely fair maps, the maps for the election that we are holding this month and for the decade ahead. And then, a funny thing happened.
<v Ira Glass>于是，新的制图规则生效了。俄亥俄州进入了一个选区划分不公后的乌托邦世界，人们本可以沟通，两极分化会缩小，至少会缩小一点点。2021年，他们第一次有机会绘制完全公平的新地图，即我们这个月要举行的选举以及未来十年的选举地图。然后，发生了一件有趣的事。

00:03:46.310 --> 00:03:52.580
<v Reporter 1>Ohio's newly drawn legislative maps have now been deemed invalid and, in fact, unconstitutional.
<v Reporter 1>俄亥俄州新绘制的立法地图现在被认定无效，事实上，被认定为违宪。

00:03:52.580 --> 00:04:17.470
<v Ira Glass>The Ohio Supreme Court said the maps drawn by Republican mapmakers were gerrymandered still. They didn't do the basic things they just enacted a constitutional amendment to do. But OK, everybody knows it's hard for old dogs to learn new tricks. Partisans will be partisans, maybe to be expected. Slapped down by the court, lawmakers got another shot. They redrew the maps. Weeks later--
<v Ira Glass>俄亥俄州最高法院表示，由共和党地图绘制者绘制的地图仍然存在选区划分不公。他们根本没有做他们刚刚通过宪法修正案要求做的基本事情。不过没关系，大家都知道老狗学不会新把戏。党派中人终究是党派中人，这也许是可以预见的。被法院驳回后，立法者们又获得了一次机会。他们重新绘制了地图。几周后——

00:04:17.470 --> 00:04:31.840
<v Reporter 2>Well, now to breaking political news tonight. Ohio's Supreme Court is sending Ohio's House and Senate back to the drawing board again. Today the Supreme Court ruled the state's redistricting maps as unconstitutional for the second time in less than a month. Well, now--
<v Reporter 2>好的，现在来关注今晚突发的政治新闻。俄亥俄州最高法院再次让俄亥俄州众议院和参议院重回制图板。今天，最高法院在一个月内第二次裁定该州的重新划分选区地图违宪。好的，现在——

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:37.720
<v Ira Glass>That was February. Lawmakers tried again to draw an ungerrymandered map. And in March--
<v Ira Glass>那是二月份的事。立法者们再次尝试绘制一份没有选区划分不公的地图。到了三月——

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:44.410
<v Reporter 3>Welcome into Action News at 4 on this St Patrick's Day. And the third time not a lucky charm for Ohio Republican mapmakers.
<v Reporter 3>欢迎收看4点的行动新闻，今天是圣帕特里克节。对俄亥俄州共和党制图者来说，第三次并不是幸运符。

00:04:44.410 --> 00:04:49.210
<v Reporter 4>The state Supreme Court overturned another set of general assembly maps late Thursday night.
<v Reporter 4>州最高法院周四深夜推翻了另一套大会选区地图。

00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:51.480
<v Ira Glass>I'm sure you see where this is going. April.
<v Ira Glass>我敢肯定你知道接下来会发生什么。四月。

00:04:51.480 --> 00:05:03.250
<v Reporter 5>Well, 10 TV news at 5 begins with several breaking news stories tonight. We start at the Ohio Supreme Court. There, the justices struck down redistricting maps as gerrymandered for the fourth time.
<v Reporter 5>好的，5点的10频道新闻今晚从几个突发新闻报道开始。我们从俄亥俄州最高法院开始。在那里，法官们第四次以选区划分不公为由驳回了重新划分选区的地图。

00:05:03.250 --> 00:05:05.590
<v Ira Glass>And then, May.
<v Ira Glass>然后是五月。

00:05:05.590 --> 00:05:09.580
<v Reporter 6>The Ohio Supreme Court rejecting legislative maps for a fifth time.
<v Reporter 6>俄亥俄州最高法院第五次否决了立法地图。

00:05:09.580 --> 00:05:38.700
<v Ira Glass>OK, yes, in all, the maps of Ohio State House were rejected five times. And then, on top of that, when it came to its representatives to the US Congress, those districts were rejected twice. And then, time ran out. There's no more time to redraw any maps. Because of all that, Ohio is going to be going to the polls this week with maps its Supreme Court has declared unconstitutional-- illegal, just deeply gerrymandered.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，是的，总共来说，俄亥俄州众议院的地图被否决了五次。此外，在涉及美国国会代表时，这些选区也两次被否决。然后，时间耗尽了。没有更多时间重新绘制任何地图了。正因为如此，俄亥俄州本周将带着最高法院宣布违宪的地图——非法的、深深陷入选区划分不公的地图——去进行投票。

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:56.890
<v Ira Glass>How did they end up here? How is that even possible? Well, that's a story I'm here to tell you today. A bunch of states are going to the polls this week with gerrymandered districts that heavily favor one side over the other. But in Ohio this year, it wasn't supposed to be that way.
<v Ira Glass>他们怎么会走到这一步？这怎么可能？嗯，这就是我今天在这里要讲的故事。本周，许多州都将带着明显倾向于一方的、经过不公正划分的选区进行投票。但在俄亥俄州，今年本不该是这样的。

00:05:56.890 --> 00:06:10.440
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago, it's "This American Life." I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us, voters.
<v Ira Glass>来自芝加哥WBEZ电台，这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。选民们，请留下来。

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:32.250
<v Ira Glass>Act one-- They made each other a pledge. Unheard of. Absurd. OK, so when I first started looking into this, one of the things I really didn't understand was how did Ohio end up with this constitutional amendment at all to fix the way they draw districts. After all, if you think about it, whichever party was in power, they had no motive at all to want to change.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕——他们互相做出了承诺。闻所未闻。荒谬。好吧，当我刚开始研究这个的时候，我真正不理解的事情之一就是俄亥俄州到底是如何最终通过这项宪法修正案来修复他们绘制选区方式的。毕竟，如果你仔细想想，无论哪个党派掌权，他们根本没有任何想要改变的动机。

00:06:32.250 --> 00:06:53.310
<v Ira Glass>Democrats, in fact, happily gerrymandered for years and years, and they killed gerrymandering reform in Ohio-- most recently, back in 2010. Lately, of course, it's been the Republicans drawing unfair districts. The majorities are so overwhelming at this point, the Senate president has said, publicly, "We can kind of do what we want," which is true.
<v Ira Glass>事实上，民主党人多年来一直乐于搞选区划分不公，并且他们在俄亥俄州扼杀了选区划分改革——最近一次是在2010年。当然，近来一直是共和党人在绘制不公平的选区。此时此刻，多数派的优势已经非常压倒性了，参议院议长甚至公开表示：“我们想做什么就可以做什么”，这确实是真的。

00:06:53.310 --> 00:07:07.680
<v Ira Glass>Republican supermajorities in Ohio pass laws like, for example, the one that banned abortion, even in the case of rape and incest. In June, you probably remember this led to a 10-year-old Ohio girl who'd been raped to have to go to Indiana for an abortion.
<v Ira Glass>俄亥俄州的共和党超级多数派通过了诸如禁止堕胎之类的法律，即使在强奸和乱伦的情况下也是如此。六月，你可能还记得，这导致一名在俄亥俄州被强奸的10岁女孩不得不去印第安纳州进行堕胎。

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:16.710
<v David Pepper>In a state that a majority supports Roe v. Wade, I dare say 5% or fewer Ohioans agree with how that 10-year-old was treated.
<v David Pepper>在一个大多数人支持罗诉韦德案的州，我敢说只有5%或更少的俄亥俄州人认同那个10岁女孩所受的待遇。

00:07:16.710 --> 00:07:29.940
<v Ira Glass>David Pepper used to run the Democratic Party in the state, but became so alarmed at the kinds of laws he saw the Republican supermajority passing and the way they rigged maps to stay in power, that he wrote a book, Laboratories of Autocracy, documenting it.
<v Ira Glass>戴维·佩珀过去曾负责该州的民主党工作，但由于对共和党超级多数派通过的法律种类以及他们操纵选区以保持权力的方式感到如此震惊，他写了一本书《专制的实验室》，记录了这一切。

00:07:29.940 --> 00:07:50.320
<v David Pepper>It's a state that only a few months ago passed a bill because they were so worried about trans athletes that said that, on demand, girls playing on sports teams and of all ages should on demand have genital inspections-- genital inspections. But that extremism does not reflect the people. Very few people would support it.
<v David Pepper>就在几个月前，该州还通过了一项法案，因为他们太担心跨性别运动员，该法案称，所有年龄段参加运动队的女孩，如果需要，应该接受生殖器检查——生殖器检查。但这种极端主义并不能反映人民的意愿。几乎没有人会支持它。

00:07:50.320 --> 00:08:14.950
<v David Pepper>Same with guns-- this is a state that the vast majorities would support background checks. The legislature passes laws where only 10% or so agree with them. And so the gerrymandering is, again, not just about a unearned majority control. It's what is fueling the extremism and behavior from politicians that I think most Americans can't understand. It's about no competition whatsoever.
<v David Pepper>枪支问题也一样——在这个州，绝大多数人会支持背景调查。而立法机关通过的法律，只有约10%的人同意。所以，选区划分不公再次重申，不仅仅是关于不劳而获的多数控制权。这才是助长政客极端主义和行为的根源，我想大多数美国人都无法理解。这是因为完全没有竞争。

00:08:14.950 --> 00:08:37.600
<v Ira Glass>So whoever's in power has good reasons to keep gerrymandering. And in Ohio, whenever anybody has tried to stop them, they've failed. It's been a long history of hard fought battles going back to the early '80s. Catherine Turcer, who runs one of the do-gooder groups that's active on this issue, Common Cause Ohio, she jumped into the fight in the 1990s when she first heard about gerrymandering and got hooked.
<v Ira Glass>所以谁掌权，谁就有充分的理由继续搞选区划分不公。在俄亥俄州，每当有人试图阻止他们时，都失败了。从80年代初开始，这就是一场艰苦战斗的悠久历史。凯瑟琳·瑟瑟经营着一个在此问题上活跃的公益组织“共同事业俄亥俄”，她在90年代第一次听说选区划分不公时就投入了这场斗争，并从此深陷其中。

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:43.970
<v Catherine Turcer>You know, at first, it just sounds like some kind of weird conspiracy, something that happens every 10 years behind closed doors, and it's--
<v Catherine Turcer>你知道，起初，这听起来就像某种奇怪的阴谋，每10年在闭门造车中发生一次，而且——

00:08:43.970 --> 00:09:18.380
<v Ira Glass>Nearly everybody you're going to hear this hour has some moment like this, where the arcane details of political redistricting took over their lives. In the '90s, Catherine gathered signatures to put a constitutional amendment on the Ohio ballot, which takes hundreds of thousands of signatures. They couldn't collect enough. She worked on the statewide campaign, and they tried again in 2005. This time, they got on the ballot, and voters rejected them horribly-- 70% to 30%. Tried again in 2012, lost again, similar margin. Catherine describes what that feels like this way.
<v Ira Glass>这一小时你听到的几乎每个人都有过这样的时刻，政治选区重划的晦涩细节接管了他们的生活。在90年代，凯瑟琳收集签名以将宪法修正案列入俄亥俄州选票，这需要数十万个签名。他们没能收集到足够多的签名。她在全州范围内开展运动，他们曾在2005年再次尝试。这一次，他们成功列入选票，但被选民以70%对30%的比例惨痛地否决了。2012年再次尝试，再次失败，票数差距相似。凯瑟琳这样描述那种感觉。

00:09:18.380 --> 00:09:23.450
<v Catherine Turcer>I had to-- I was in a really terrible car accident, and I had to relearn to walk. And--
<v Catherine Turcer>我不得不——我经历了一场非常可怕的车祸，我不得不重新学习走路。并且——

00:09:23.450 --> 00:09:23.720
<v Ira Glass>Wow.
<v Ira Glass>哇。

00:09:23.720 --> 00:09:35.450
<v Catherine Turcer>--I've recovered from cancer. I have gotten a divorce. None of them are as awful as losing a ballot measure.
<v Catherine Turcer>——我从癌症中恢复过来。我离过婚。这些都没有输掉一项投票提案那么糟糕。

00:09:35.450 --> 00:09:39.380
<v Ira Glass>Wait, a divorce-- I just want to go through the list again. A divorce--
<v Ira Glass>等等，离婚——我只是想再过一遍这个清单。离婚——

00:09:39.380 --> 00:09:40.010
<v Catherine Turcer>[LAUGHS]
<v Catherine Turcer>（笑声）

00:09:40.010 --> 00:09:44.240
<v Ira Glass>--cancer, and getting in a terrible car accident where you have to learn to walk afterwards--
<v Ira Glass>——癌症，还有经历一场可怕的车祸，之后不得不重新学习走路——

00:09:44.240 --> 00:09:44.570
<v Catherine Turcer>Yes.
<v Catherine Turcer>是的。

00:09:44.570 --> 00:09:49.130
<v Ira Glass>--not as bad as losing a ballot measure.
<v Ira Glass>——没有输掉一项投票提案那么糟糕。

00:09:49.130 --> 00:09:55.510
<v Catherine Turcer>Because you just have to do it all over again.
<v Catherine Turcer>因为你只是不得不重新再做一遍。

00:09:55.510 --> 00:10:02.050
<v Ira Glass>And why did you lose so badly, like if this is just sort of sensible good government, let's have fair district lines?
<v Ira Glass>既然这只是某种明智的良好治理，让我们有公平的选区界线，为什么你们会输得那么惨？

00:10:02.050 --> 00:10:33.490
<v Catherine Turcer>So the opposition-- oh my gosh, they spent so much money-- just talked about how crazy the redistricting map would be, and then they spent millions of dollars-- basically things like faceless bureaucrats are going to draw the district lines. Oh, and they're going to spend unlimited amounts of money on this mapmaking process. You have no idea how much money they're going to be spending.
<v Catherine Turcer>所以反对派——天哪，他们花了很多很多钱——只是谈论重新划分选区地图会有多疯狂，然后他们花了数百万美元——基本上是说一些无名的官僚要来绘制选区界线之类的东西。哦，而且他们将在这次地图绘制过程中花费无限量的钱。你根本不知道他们会花多少钱。

00:10:33.490 --> 00:11:00.390
<v Ira Glass>So the year all this partisanship ended, or at least paused, and all the parties sat down to fix mapmaking in Ohio, was 2014. And there are a bunch of reasons that it happened that year. One big reason-- the map that the Republicans had just drawn in the last round of map making was so undeniably lopsided, some of the laws the new majority started passing were more inflammatory than in the past, especially this measure to try to bust up public unions, which led to protests and a ballot measure.
<v Ira Glass>所以，这种党派之争结束，或者至少暂停，所有党派坐下来修复俄亥俄州地图绘制的那一年是2014年。那一年发生这种情况有很多原因。一个主要原因——共和党人在上一轮地图绘制中刚刚绘制的地图太不可否认地不平衡了，新多数派开始通过的一些法律比过去更具煽动性，尤其是这项试图瓦解公共工会的措施，这导致了抗议和一项投票提案。

00:11:00.390 --> 00:11:20.730
<v Ira Glass>After that, a lot more people took notice of how weird the districts looked. Republicans started worrying that the Democrats and the good government groups were going to bring a ballot measure to change the constitution and end gerrymandering. And of course, they defeated these kinds of measures in the past, but this time, there was one new factor that was going to make it harder.
<v Ira Glass>在那之后，更多人注意到选区看起来是多么奇怪。共和党人开始担心民主党人和良政团体将提出一项投票提案来修改宪法并结束选区划分不公。当然，他们过去曾挫败过这类提案，但这一次，有一个新的因素会使其变得更加困难。

00:11:20.730 --> 00:11:51.240
<v Ira Glass>And to explain that part, let me just introduce you to the mastermind behind the Republican strategy in Ohio when it comes to redistricting and map drawing, one of the leading Republicans in the state, Matt Huffman. Back when he and I spoke, I was still trying to figure out who was the person who first had the idea for the 2015 constitutional amendment to curtail gerrymandering, like who is the person who got everybody else on board. Somebody had told me that it was Catherine Turcer, the woman from Common Cause. Matt Huffman was very clear on this point.
<v Ira Glass>为了解释那一部分，让我为你介绍一下俄亥俄州共和党在重新划分选区和绘制地图方面的战略策划者，该州领先的共和党人之一，马特·赫夫曼。当我们交谈时，我还在试图找出谁是第一个提出2015年宪法修正案以遏制选区划分不公想法的人，比如是谁让其他人加入进来的。有人告诉我那是“共同事业”组织的凯瑟琳·瑟瑟。马特·赫夫曼在这一点上非常明确。

00:11:51.240 --> 00:12:02.970
<v Matt Huffman>It was not Catherine Turcer. It was not Catherine Turcer. It was a young state representative Matt Huffman who introduced this, and I'll tell you why-- personally, why I ended up doing that.
<v Matt Huffman>那不是凯瑟琳·瑟瑟。那不是凯瑟琳·瑟瑟。是年轻的州众议员马特·赫夫曼提出了这个建议，我会告诉你我个人为什么最终会那样做。

00:12:02.970 --> 00:12:15.900
<v Ira Glass>That checks out, by the way, with people who are close to the process. Matt Huffman says all this began after he fought to stop one of those ballot initiatives to get rid of gerrymandering. This is the one in 2012, the second one in 10 years.
<v Ira Glass>顺便说一句，这与接近该过程的人士的说法相符。马特·赫夫曼说这一切始于他为阻止其中一项旨在消除选区划分不公的投票倡议而奋斗之后。这是2012年的那一次，10年来的第二次。

00:12:15.900 --> 00:12:32.100
<v Matt Huffman>People spend a lot of money saying, yes, no, this is outrageous, gerrymandering. And I went around the state, and I raised money and lots of folks said, gee, we seem to be doing this a lot. We'll give you money, but isn't there anything you can do so we don't have to keep having this fight every three or four years?
<v Matt Huffman>人们花了很多钱说，是的，不，这太离谱了，选区划分不公。我走遍全州，筹集资金，很多人说，天哪，我们似乎经常做这件事。我们会给你钱，但没有什么办法能让我们不用每三四年就得一直进行这种争斗吗？

00:12:32.100 --> 00:12:42.060
<v Ira Glass>Yeah, the thing that I had heard was that the Chamber of Commerce said, like, we shouldn't be spending our money on this. We should be spending money on races and getting people to win. And that's right?
<v Ira Glass>是的，我听说商会说过，我们不应该把钱花在这上面。我们应该把钱花在竞选上，让人们获胜。那是对的吗？

00:12:42.060 --> 00:13:09.330
<v Matt Huffman>Yeah, well, that's a-- I mean, I'm not recalling a specific conversation in the Chamber of Commerce, but those, typically Republican, groups. People did say to me in 2012, we're not going to write checks for this anymore. Go solve the problem. Can't you come up with some compromise? We almost had one in 2010, the Republicans in the Senate. And I said, yeah, but the Democrats wouldn't do it. Well, maybe they'll do it now. So, OK, I'm going to take a shot at it.
<v Matt Huffman>是的，嗯，这是——我的意思是我不记得商会有过什么具体的谈话，但那些通常是共和党团体。人们确实在2012年对我说了，我们不会再为此开支票了。去解决这个问题吧。难道你就不能想出一个妥协方案吗？我们曾在2010年差点有一个，参议院的共和党人。我说，是的，但民主党人不肯做。好吧，也许他们现在肯了。所以，好吧，我要试一试。

00:13:09.330 --> 00:13:24.240
<v Ira Glass>Huffman sees himself as somebody who almost compulsively wants to jump at a problem and try to solve it, and even his opponents say that he is an enormously creative legislator. Back then, he'd just been through hearings where Democrats and Republicans both talked about what they'd want to see in a constitutional reform of gerrymandering.
<v Ira Glass>赫夫曼认为自己是一个几乎强迫性地想要扑向问题并试图解决它的人，甚至他的反对者也说他是一位极具创造力的立法者。当时，他刚刚经历过听证会，民主党人和共和党人都谈论过他们希望在选区划分不公的宪法改革中看到什么。

00:13:24.240 --> 00:13:45.990
<v Ira Glass>And he and everybody else saw possibilities for compromise. So five negotiators went into a room in the State House for secret talks that one of them told me lasted 39 hours. Huffman and the bigwigs in the parties signed on, and the two sides came to relatively quick agreement-- on some of the rules, anyway-- for map drawing that now appear in the Constitution.
<v Ira Glass>他和所有其他人都看到了妥协的可能性。于是五名谈判代表进入州议会大厦进行秘密会谈，其中一人告诉我持续了39个小时。赫夫曼和党内大佬们签了字，双方在相对较短的时间内达成了协议——至少在地图绘制的一些规则上——这些规则现在出现在宪法中。

00:13:45.990 --> 00:14:05.070
<v Ira Glass>Districts have to be as compact as possible. They shouldn't sprawl out in weird shapes. Whenever possible, if there's a township or county, it should all sit in one district, or if it's too big for that, in two districts, with as few divisions in it as possible. I was told by other negotiators that last item was very important to Matt Huffman during the negotiations.
<v Ira Glass>选区必须尽可能紧凑。它们不应该以奇怪的形状蔓延。只要有可能，如果有一个乡镇或县，它应该全部坐落在一个选区内，或者如果它太大了，就在两个选区内，并尽可能减少分裂。其他谈判代表告诉我，最后一项在谈判期间对马特·赫夫曼非常重要。

00:14:05.070 --> 00:14:22.440
<v Ira Glass>And when I talked to him, he said that particular item was helpful to him personally as the guy in charge of drawing the Republican political maps, and not because it helped him fight Democrats. I thought this actually gave a fascinating glimpse into what it is like to be a political boss like him, trying to keep his allies in line and on the same page.
<v Ira Glass>当我与他交谈时，他说那个特别的条款对他个人作为负责绘制共和党政治地图的人很有帮助，并不是因为它帮助他打击民主党。我认为这实际上让人着迷地一窥像他这样的政治大佬是什么样的，试图让他的盟友保持一致并保持在同一页面上。

00:14:22.440 --> 00:14:38.410
<v Matt Huffman>Much of the problem with redistricting is not party versus party. It's intraparty wrangling and ridiculous demands made by legislators about having this county in their district-- or their hometown.
<v Matt Huffman>重新划分选区的大部分问题不是政党对政党。而是党内争论以及立法者关于在他们的选区拥有这个县——或者他们的家乡——的荒谬要求。

00:14:38.410 --> 00:14:38.790
<v Ira Glass>Mm-hmm.
<v Ira Glass>嗯嗯。

00:14:38.790 --> 00:15:09.360
<v Matt Huffman>And it's not-- it's not fair. It's not realistic. I want to have that township because that's where my daughter lives or my restaurant is there, or I at least want to be included in that congressional district because-- and this happened because I may want to run for Congress because I think that guy is going to retire. We can say, we're not allowed to do that because we're not allowed to split townships in half. And it's an answer to a lot of bad behavior on both sides of the aisle.
<v Matt Huffman>而且这不是——这不公平。这不现实。我想要那个乡镇，因为我女儿住在那里，或者我的餐馆在那里，或者我至少想被包括在那个国会选区内，因为——而且这发生了——因为我可能想竞选国会议员，因为我觉得那个家伙要退休了。我们可以说，我们不被允许这样做，因为我们不允许把乡镇一分为二。这是对两党内部很多不良行为的一种回答。

00:15:09.360 --> 00:15:22.140
<v Ira Glass>Meanwhile, what Democrats and good government groups wanted in the constitution was something to guarantee that the district maps would not favor one party over another. And they knew it wasn't enough just to say the words, "You can't favor one party over another."
<v Ira Glass>与此同时，民主党人和良政团体在宪法中想要的是保证选区地图不会偏袒任何一个政党的东西。他们知道，仅仅说“你不能偏袒任何一个政党”这句话是不够的。

00:15:22.140 --> 00:15:38.510
<v Ira Glass>Florida put that wording into its constitution, and it was vague enough that it led to years of lawsuits. So these people in Ohio wanted some kind of measurement in the constitution, some way to determine whether a map was fair or not that everybody agreed to. Catherine explains what they came up with.
<v Ira Glass>佛罗里达州将其措辞纳入了宪法，但它太模糊了，导致了多年的诉讼。所以俄亥俄州的这些人想要宪法中的某种衡量标准，某种每个人都同意的确定地图是否公平的方法。凯瑟琳解释了他们想出的办法。

00:15:38.510 --> 00:16:00.720
<v Catherine Turcer>Essentially, what's in the Ohio constitution is that we look at the statewide elections over the past decade. So in 10 years, those statewide races-- and you can think of president. You can think of senator, governor, et cetera. You take all of those. You put them together, meaning you add up the Democratic votes, and you add up the Republican votes.
<v Catherine Turcer>本质上，俄亥俄州宪法的内容是我们查看过去十年的全州选举。所以10年来，那些全州性的竞选——你可以想到总统。你可以想到参议员、州长等等。你把所有这些都拿出来。你把它们放在一起，这意味着你把民主党的选票加起来，把共和党的选票加起来。

00:16:00.720 --> 00:16:27.450
<v Ira Glass>I mean, you do that in Ohio, you see that 46% of the votes that people cast were for Democrats over the last 10 years. 54% were for Republicans. And so 46% of its state house districts should be districts with more Democrats in them, and 54% of those districts should have more Republicans. One of the five negotiators, former political science professor Richard Gunther, came up with this idea, which he calls proportional representation.
<v Ira Glass>我的意思是，你在俄亥俄州这样做，你看到过去10年来人们投下的选票中，46%是投给民主党人的。54%是投给共和党人的。所以46%的州议会选区应该是民主党人较多的选区，而54%的选区应该有更多的共和党人。五名谈判代表之一，前政治学教授理查德·冈瑟，提出了这个想法，他称之为比例代表制。

00:16:27.450 --> 00:16:40.130
<v Richard Gunther>Meaning that the share of seats leaning towards one party or another should closely correspond to the share of votes cast for those two parties. So you're starting from a level playing field.
<v Richard Gunther>这意味着倾向于某一个党派的席位份额应该与这两个政党获得的选票份额紧密对应。所以你从一个公平的竞争环境开始。

00:16:40.130 --> 00:16:42.110
<v Ira Glass>So you invent this thing.
<v Ira Glass>所以你发明了这个东西。

00:16:42.110 --> 00:16:46.850
<v Richard Gunther>Yep. It is not in any constitution of any state in the US.
<v Richard Gunther>是的。它不在美国任何一个州的任何宪法中。

00:16:46.850 --> 00:16:53.460
<v Ira Glass>Any state but Ohio.
<v Ira Glass>除了俄亥俄州之外的任何州。

00:16:53.460 --> 00:17:05.810
<v Ira Glass>When voters overwhelmingly approved the new rules, the Democrats and good government types were cautiously optimistic, like Catherine Turcer. She said they all knew it wasn't perfect. They made compromises. It was the only way to get it passed.
<v Ira Glass>当选民以压倒性多数批准新规则时，民主党人和良政类型的人持谨慎乐观态度，比如凯瑟琳·瑟瑟。她说他们都知道这并不完美。他们做出了妥协。这是通过它的唯一途径。

00:17:05.810 --> 00:17:15.200
<v Catherine Turcer>There might be a few games, but that these elected officials would do their best to meet the rules.
<v Catherine Turcer>可能会有一些博弈，但这些民选官员会尽其所能遵守规则。

00:17:15.200 --> 00:17:23.090
<v Ira Glass>I understand that, but emotionally, did you just feel like, we did it? We had this goal, and we and we won. Did you feel like that?
<v Ira Glass>我理解，但在情感上，你是否觉得，我们做到了？我们有了这个目标，并且我们赢了。你有这种感觉吗？

00:17:23.090 --> 00:17:41.770
<v Catherine Turcer>Oh my gosh. Not only did I think we won, I was like, I could retire. It was like, oh my goodness, there's like light at the end of the tunnel. And I feel sad for that girl, you know, that person.
<v Catherine Turcer>天哪。我不仅认为我们赢了，我还觉得，我可以退休了。就像是，哦，天哪，隧道尽头有光了。而且我为那个女孩感到难过，你知道，那个人。

00:17:41.770 --> 00:17:44.410
<v Ira Glass>You feel sad for 2015 Catherine Turcer.
<v Ira Glass>你为2015年的凯瑟琳·瑟瑟感到难过。

00:17:44.410 --> 00:18:03.070
<v Catherine Turcer>[LAUGHS] Yes, 2015 Catherine Turcer who believed that we had set the stage for truly meaningful elections.
<v Catherine Turcer>（笑声）是的，那个相信我们已经为真正有意义的选举奠定了基础的2015年凯瑟琳·瑟瑟。

00:18:03.070 --> 00:18:18.640
<v Ira Glass>Coming up-- what goes wrong when Ohio tries out its new constitutional amendment and draws its very first maps under the rules? That's in a minute, from Chicago Public Radio, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来——当俄亥俄州尝试其新的宪法修正案并根据规则绘制其第一张地图时，出了什么问题？一分钟后，来自芝加哥公共广播电台，当我们的节目继续时。

00:18:18.640 --> 00:18:40.500
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today's program-- Mapmaker, Mapmaker, Make Me a Map, the story of why Ohio is going to the polls this week with election maps the state's own Supreme Court says violate the state's constitution. We have arrived at Act Two of our show. Act Two-- Sunrise, Sunset slash Is This the Little Constitutional Amendment I Carried?
<v Ira Glass>这是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天的节目——制图师，制图师，给我画一张地图，关于为什么俄亥俄州本周要带着该州最高法院称违反州宪法的选举地图去投票的故事。我们已经到达了节目的第二幕。第二幕——日出，日落，斜线，这是我背负的那份小宪法修正案吗？

00:18:40.500 --> 00:18:54.180
<v Ira Glass>OK, so in 2021, Ohioans sat down to make the electoral maps for the election that we're holding this week, using their new constitutional amendment for the very first time, taking it out for a spin. And as you already know, what unfolded was kind of a disaster.
<v Ira Glass>好的，所以2021年，俄亥俄州人坐下来为我们本周要举行的选举制作选举地图，这是他们第一次使用新的宪法修正案，拿它去试运行。正如你已经知道的那样，展开的情况简直是一场灾难。

00:18:54.180 --> 00:19:09.450
<v Ira Glass>They had to make three maps in all. One of those was defining congressional districts for the US House of Representatives, then two maps for the Ohio State House, one for the Ohio Senate, one for the Ohio House of Representatives. OK, so three maps, similar things happen with all three.
<v Ira Glass>他们总共不得不制作三张地图。其中一张是定义美国众议院的国会选区，然后是两张俄亥俄州议会的地图，一张是俄亥俄州参议院的，一张是俄亥俄州众议院的。好的，所以三张地图，三张都发生了类似的事情。

00:19:09.450 --> 00:19:27.720
<v Ira Glass>And I'm going to tell you the story of what happened with the map for the Ohio House of Representatives because it was the most bitterly divisive. And also, there are things in gerrymandering that usually happen all the time around the country behind closed doors that here play out in public. And it took some pretty shocking turns along the way.
<v Ira Glass>我要告诉你关于俄亥俄州众议院地图发生的事情的故事，因为它是最令人感到痛苦的分歧。而且，选区划分不公中通常在全国各地闭门发生的事情，在这里在公开场合上演了。并且在过程中出现了一些相当令人震惊的转折。

00:19:27.720 --> 00:19:36.540
<v Mike DeWine>And I call this meeting to order. We'll now proceed to the presentation of a member appointments to the Ohio redistricting commission.
<v Mike DeWine>现在我宣布会议开始。我们现在将进行俄亥俄州选区重划委员会成员任命的陈述。

00:19:36.540 --> 00:19:58.260
<v Ira Glass>The new constitutional amendment created a redistricting commission with seven members to draw the state's electoral maps. This was their first meeting in August of last year. Seven members mandated by the amendment were some of the most powerful people in the state-- the governor, secretary of state, and auditor, and then from the state legislature, two Republican lawmakers and two Democrats.
<v Ira Glass>新的宪法修正案成立了一个有七名成员的选区重划委员会来绘制该州的选举地图。这是他们去年八月的第一次会议。修正案规定的七名成员是该州一些最有权势的人——州长、州务卿和审计长，然后来自州议会，两名共和党议员和两名民主党议员。

00:19:58.260 --> 00:20:19.980
<v Ira Glass>Matt Huffman, the Republican who was behind the amendment's creation, he was on the committee, still calling the shots. He was also by this time a state senator and president of the Ohio Senate. Vernon Sykes was the leading Democrat on the commission and its co-chair. He'd been the Democratic party's main negotiator, Huffman's counterpart when they were drafting the constitutional amendment.
<v Ira Glass>马特·赫夫曼，这个修正案创建背后的共和党人，他在委员会中，仍然掌握着指挥权。到这时他也是一名州参议员和俄亥俄州参议院议长。弗农·赛克斯是委员会的首席民主党人及其联合主席。他是民主党的主要谈判代表，当他们起草宪法修正案时，他是赫夫曼的对手。

00:20:19.980 --> 00:20:35.520
<v Ira Glass>Sykes is such a hardcore anti-gerrymandering guy that in his first term in office back in 1983, he went to the Democrats, who at that point were running things-- they were the majority. He went to the leaders of his own party and told them it was unfair the way that they were drawing the maps. They should change.
<v Ira Glass>赛克斯是一个如此坚定的反选区划分不公的家伙，以至于在1983年的第一个任期内，他去找了当时掌权的民主党人——他们是多数派。他去找自己政党的领导人，并告诉他们他们划分地图的方式是不公平的。他们应该改变。

00:20:35.520 --> 00:20:40.980
<v Vernon Sykes>It was a difficult row to hoe and really couldn't convince them to do that.
<v Vernon Sykes>这是一条艰难的道路，真的无法说服他们那样做。

00:20:40.980 --> 00:20:44.070
<v Ira Glass>Like, did they say, like, are you crazy?
<v Ira Glass>就像，他们说了，比如，你疯了吗？

00:20:44.070 --> 00:20:56.080
<v Vernon Sykes>Yeah, that was basically the terminology that was used. They thought I was naive and young and inexperienced and-- and I would get over it.
<v Vernon Sykes>是的，基本上用的就是那个术语。他们认为我太天真、年轻且缺乏经验——而且我会克服它的。

00:20:56.080 --> 00:21:13.930
<v Ira Glass>Obviously, he did not get over it, and now here he sat, four decades later. In all, it was five Republicans on the committee, two Democrats. Mapmaking got a late start in Ohio because the new census data was late. But then, finally, on September 9 of last year--
<v Ira Glass>显然，他没有克服它，现在他坐在这里，四十年后。总而言之，委员会里有五名共和党人，两名民主党人。俄亥俄州的制图开始得比较晚，因为新的人口普查数据晚了。但随后，终于，在去年9月9日——

00:21:13.930 --> 00:21:14.680
<v Matt Huffman>Mr. Co-Chair?
<v Matt Huffman>联合主席先生？

00:21:14.680 --> 00:21:15.760
<v Vernon Sykes>Yes.
<v Vernon Sykes>是的。

00:21:15.760 --> 00:21:29.170
<v Matt Huffman>I would-- pursuant to Rule 10 of the commission, I would like to present to the commission a proposed General Assembly district plan for all 99 seats of the Ohio House of Representatives and all 33 seats of the Ohio Senate.
<v Matt Huffman>根据委员会第10条规则，我想向委员会提交一份拟议的俄亥俄州众议院所有99个席位和俄亥俄州参议院所有33个席位的大会选区计划。

00:21:29.170 --> 00:21:53.230
<v Ira Glass>That's Matt Huffman proposing maps that have been cooked up by the Republicans on their own. The new constitutional amendment actually calls for everybody on the commission to draft this map together, but Republicans said that the census data came in too late for that-- no time to collaborate. Democrats and the government types said that was nonsense. So this first map was like a cannon shot launching the war that followed.
<v Ira Glass>那是马特·赫夫曼提议共和党人自己炮制的地图。新的宪法修正案实际上要求委员会里的每个人一起起草这张地图，但共和党人说人口普查数据来得太晚了——没有时间合作。民主党人和政府类型的人说那是无稽之谈。所以第一张地图就像发动随后的战争的炮击。

00:21:53.230 --> 00:21:56.980
<v Matt Huffman>Thank you. At this time, we are ready for the presentation.
<v Matt Huffman>谢谢。现在，我们准备好进行陈述了。

00:21:56.980 --> 00:22:15.730
<v Ira Glass>The single most important thing about the map that anybody would want to know-- did it favor one party more than another? The Republicans didn't give any information about that at all in this first hearing. And mind you, they were doing all this at the very last minute. There were only six days before the constitution said they had to approve some map.
<v Ira Glass>关于这张地图，任何人想知道的最重要的一件事——它是否比另一个政党更偏袒某一方？在这次第一次听证会上，共和党人根本没有提供任何相关信息。请注意，他们是在最后一分钟才做所有这些事情的。宪法规定他们必须批准某张地图，距离截止日期只有六天了。

00:22:15.730 --> 00:22:41.890
<v Ira Glass>Given the way the constitution was written, like we said earlier, 54% of Ohioans had been voting Republican over the previous decade, so most people figured that 54% of the districts should have Republican majorities. When the numbers were finally revealed, Republicans got a lot more than that. The map gave Republicans between 64% and 70% of all the districts. I asked a few people who are at these hearings to talk me through what they were like.
<v Ira Glass>鉴于宪法的书写方式，就像我们之前说的，过去十年来，54%的俄亥俄州人一直在投票支持共和党，所以大多数人认为54%的选区应该是共和党多数。当数据最终公布时，共和党人得到的远不止这些。地图给了共和党人所有选区中64%到70%的份额。我问了几个参加这些听证会的人，让他们带我走一遍听证会是什么样的。

00:22:41.890 --> 00:23:03.310
<v Ira Glass>Katy Shanahan was there working with a group called the National Democratic Redistricting Committee. She personally had locked in on gerrymandering as her issue when she was in law school. She wrote a thesis evaluating how different states draw their maps and which are the most fair. Like everybody I spoke with, she expected the Republicans to try to gerrymander, you know, like, a little. But this first map--
<v Ira Glass>凯蒂·沙纳汉在那里与一个名为“全国民主党选区重划委员会”的组织一起工作。当她在法学院时，她个人就锁定了选区划分不公作为她的议题。她写了一篇论文，评估不同州如何绘制地图以及哪些是最公平的。像我采访的所有人一样，她预料到共和党人会尝试搞选区划分不公，你知道，搞一点点。但这张第一张地图——

00:23:03.310 --> 00:23:28.720
<v Katy Shanahan>It's sort of impressive, [LAUGHS] in a bad way, that they managed to draw maps that were even worse than the ones that we had in place the last 10 years. After having helped write and pass a constitutional reform meant to curb that exact type of gerrymandering, the audacity of their action is breathtaking, no matter how predictable.
<v Katy Shanahan>这有点令人印象深刻，（笑声）以一种糟糕的方式，他们设法绘制了比我们过去10年所拥有的地图更糟糕的地图。在帮助起草和通过旨在遏制那种确切类型的选区划分不公的宪法改革之后，他们行为的厚颜无耻令人叹为观止，无论它多么可预测。

00:23:28.720 --> 00:23:39.520
<v Matt Huffman>The simple fact is that there are a lot of opinions about what that portion of the constitution means. For example--
<v Matt Huffman>简单的事实是，关于宪法的那部分意味着什么，有很多意见。例如——

00:23:39.520 --> 00:24:01.570
<v Ira Glass>During the hearings, Senator Huffman explained the number of Republican districts this way. He said that, under the constitution, the number of districts that each party gets is supposed to reflect the results in statewide elections over the previous 10 years. And results is the word the constitution uses-- results. But Huffman says, there are different ways that you can measure election results.
<v Ira Glass>在听证会期间，赫夫曼参议员这样解释共和党选区的数量。他说，根据宪法，每个政党获得的选区数量应该反映过去10年来全州选举的结果。而“结果”这个词就是宪法使用的词——结果。但赫夫曼说，衡量选举结果有不同的方式。

00:24:01.570 --> 00:24:20.920
<v Ira Glass>Like, for instance, you can count the number of votes cast. If you do that, then Republicans won 54% of the votes, sure. But he said you could also count the number of races won-- governor, lieutenant governor, senator, all that. If you count the number of races, if you do it that way, Republicans won 81% of the races.
<v Ira Glass>比如，你可以计算投下的选票数量。如果你这样做，那么共和党赢得了54%的选票，当然。但他说是的，你也可以计算赢得的竞选次数——州长、副州长、参议员，所有这些。如果你计算竞选次数，如果你那样做，共和党赢得了81%的竞选。

00:24:20.920 --> 00:24:22.540
<v Ira Glass>Again, Katy Shanahan.
<v Ira Glass>还是凯蒂·沙纳汉。

00:24:22.540 --> 00:24:38.020
<v Katy Shanahan>And the explanation was, essentially, so we really could have gotten way worse maps, right? We could have drawn maps that gave us 81% of the seats, and that would have been fair. But we didn't do that, and isn't that so gracious of us to do that? And that was their explanation.
<v Katy Shanahan>而解释基本上是，所以我们真的可以得到更糟糕的地图，对吧？我们可以绘制给我们81%席位的地图，那将是公平的。但我们没有那样做，我们这样做难道不是很仁慈吗？这就是他们的解释。

00:24:38.020 --> 00:24:40.160
<v Ira Glass>Yeah, what did you think of that argument?
<v Ira Glass>是的，你觉得那个论点怎么样？

00:24:40.160 --> 00:24:58.870
<v Katy Shanahan>Well, excuse my French, but it's horseshit. Election results, I mean, I guess you could read that multiple ways. But to me, what that reads is, how have Ohioans voted in all of those races? If you're only counting who's won those elections, you're ignoring all of the other voters.
<v Katy Shanahan>嗯，请原谅我的法语，但那是胡扯。选举结果，我的意思是，我想你可以用多种方式解读它。但对我来说，那解读为，俄亥俄州人在所有这些竞选中是如何投票的？如果你只计算谁赢得了这些选举，你就忽略了所有其他选民。

00:24:58.870 --> 00:25:21.600
<v Katy Shanahan>So there are some of those statewide races that were decided by a couple percentage points, which means you're essentially ignoring half of the voters who showed up to vote in those elections, which is not what I think what statewide preferences of the voters of Ohio means. It doesn't mean only count who's won those races and disregard everyone else who voted.
<v Katy Shanahan>所以其中一些全州竞选是以几个百分点决定的，这意味着你基本上忽略了那些参加这些选举投票的选民中的一半，这在我看来并不是俄亥俄州选民全州偏好的意思。它并不意味着只计算谁赢了这些竞选，而无视所有其他投票的人。

00:25:21.600 --> 00:25:36.690
<v Ira Glass>I will say, the Democrats and good government types who negotiated the terms of this constitutional amendment with Matt Huffman found this interpretation of the word "results" to be so annoying. Like, they were there when this was written. Again, Catherine Turcer.
<v Ira Glass>我要说，与马特·赫夫曼谈判这些宪法修正案条款的民主党人和良政类型的人发现对“结果”这个词的这种解释令人恼火。就像，当这被书写时，他们在场。还是凯瑟琳·瑟瑟。

00:25:36.690 --> 00:25:46.710
<v Catherine Turcer>And the notion that Matt Huffman didn't understand what representational fairness is is absolutely ludicrous. There were long conversations about it for years, really.
<v Catherine Turcer>而且认为马特·赫夫曼不理解什么是代表性公平的概念绝对是荒谬的。确实有多年关于它的长期对话。

00:25:46.710 --> 00:25:59.700
<v Ira Glass>Richard Gunther said the same thing. Remember, he was one of the five negotiators who hammered out the terms of the amendment. He says, whenever they talked about election results, it was always about the number of votes, never about the number of races won.
<v Ira Glass>理查德·冈瑟说了同样的话。记住，他是敲定修正案条款的五名谈判代表之一。他说，每当他们谈论选举结果时，总是关于选票数量，从来不是关于赢得的竞选数量。

00:25:59.700 --> 00:26:11.470
<v Richard Gunther>No, that was never mentioned. And in fact, I've been a professional political scientist for five decades, and I've never seen election data used in that bizarre fashion.
<v Richard Gunther>不，那从来没有被提到过。事实上，我当了五十年的职业政治学家，我从未见过选举数据以那种怪异的方式被使用。

00:26:11.470 --> 00:26:22.060
<v Ira Glass>Matt Huffman totally sticks by his guns in this one. He told me the word in the constitution is "results." This notion that it means counting votes and not offices won--
<v Ira Glass>马特·赫夫曼完全坚持他在这件事上的立场。他告诉我宪法中的词是“结果”。这个认为它意味着计算选票而不是赢得的职位的概念——

00:26:22.060 --> 00:26:29.950
<v Matt Huffman>Well, why does the results mean that? Well, because I want it to? Because it's better for me? Well, those aren't really reasons. Well, you know--
<v Matt Huffman>嗯，为什么结果意味着那个？嗯，因为我想要它那样？因为它对我更好？嗯，那些并不是真正的理由。嗯，你知道——

00:26:29.950 --> 00:26:35.650
<v Ira Glass>But they're saying-- they're saying, just, that's what everybody talked about back then. Nobody talked about counting the number of offices.
<v Ira Glass>但他们说——他们说，只是，那是每个人当时讨论的内容。没有人讨论计算职位的数量。

00:26:35.650 --> 00:26:48.540
<v Matt Huffman>Yeah, then it should be in the constitution. This is like the agreement, right? We enter into a settlement agreement to settle our lawsuit, and later on, you say, well, on the side, you said you were paying court costs. I never said that.
<v Matt Huffman>是的，那么它应该写在宪法里。这就像是协议，对吧？我们订立了一份和解协议来解决我们的诉讼，后来，你说，好吧，私下里，你说过你要支付法庭费用。我从来没说过那样的话。

00:26:48.540 --> 00:26:48.790
<v Ira Glass>Mm-hmm.
<v Ira Glass>嗯嗯。

00:26:48.790 --> 00:26:59.620
<v Matt Huffman>Or on the side, I was supposed to get an extra $10,000. Remember, you mentioned it to me just before we signed the document? No. And so that's why we have the constitution and the votes--
<v Matt Huffman>或者私下里，我应该得到额外的10,000美元。记得，你就在我们签署文件之前对我提过它？不。所以这就是为什么我们有宪法和投票——

00:26:59.620 --> 00:27:03.940
<v Ira Glass>And you're saying the language-- the language-- the language doesn't specify. So it could be either one.
<v Ira Glass>而且你是在说语言——语言——语言没有指定。所以它可能是任何一个。

00:27:03.940 --> 00:27:05.280
<v Matt Huffman>Right.
<v Matt Huffman>对。

00:27:05.280 --> 00:27:15.240
<v Frank LaRose>Yeah, Mr. President, so I've been trying to understand, as we've been talking to members of your staff and you yourself, how you believe that your--
<v Frank LaRose>是的，总统先生，所以我一直在试图理解，当我们和你的员工以及你自己谈话时，你如何相信你的——

00:27:15.240 --> 00:27:34.790
<v Ira Glass>In hearings over this, even the other Republicans on the redistricting commission seem to have trouble with Matt Huffman's rationale for this map. This is Ohio Secretary of State Frank LaRose, and as he is saying these words, the committee is in the final hour-- like, literally, it's the final hour before the constitutional deadline for approving a map. It's minutes before midnight on September 15.
<v Ira Glass>在关于这个的听证会上，即使是选区重划委员会的其他共和党人似乎对马特·赫夫曼这张地图的基本原理也有困难。这是俄亥俄州州务卿弗兰克·拉罗斯，当他说出这些话时，委员会正处于最后时刻——真的，离宪法批准地图的截止日期只有最后一个小时了。这是9月15日午夜前几分钟。

00:27:34.790 --> 00:27:47.010
<v Ira Glass>LaRose is complaining that Huffman and his staff didn't even share information with other Republicans on the committee, never gave any explanation until then for why there were so many more Republican districts than the constitution seemed to allow.
<v Ira Glass>拉罗斯抱怨赫夫曼和他的员工甚至没有与委员会的其他共和党人分享信息，直到那时才给出任何解释，说明为什么共和党选区比宪法似乎允许的多得多。

00:27:47.010 --> 00:27:55.680
<v Frank LaRose>I, for one, have been asking for the rationale for days. Is there a reason why that wasn't shared with us until now?
<v Frank LaRose>我本人，几天来一直都在询问基本原理。有什么理由直到现在才与我们分享吗？

00:27:55.680 --> 00:28:04.020
<v Ira Glass>LaRose texted his chief of staff, quote, "This rationale is asinine. I should vote no." This came out in depositions a month later.
<v Ira Glass>拉罗斯给他的办公厅主任发了短信，引用“这个基本原理是愚蠢的。我应该投反对票。”这一个月后在证词中被披露。

00:28:04.020 --> 00:28:09.000
<v Matt Huffman>Further discussion on the motion. Governor DeWine?
<v Matt Huffman>对该动议的进一步讨论。德温州长？

00:28:09.000 --> 00:28:13.590
<v Ira Glass>Ohio's Republican Governor Mike DeWine also seemed concerned about how far Huffman had gone.
<v Ira Glass>俄亥俄州的共和党州长迈克·德温似乎也对赫夫曼走得有多远感到担忧。

00:28:13.590 --> 00:28:44.190
<v Mike DeWine>Thank you, Mr. Chairman. I am deeply disappointed where we are tonight. I know-- I know that this committee could have produced a more clearly constitutional bill, but that's not the bill that we have in front of us. And I'm sorry we did not do that.
<v Mike DeWine>谢谢，主席先生。我对我们今晚所处的位置感到非常失望。我知道——我知道这个委员会本可以产生一个更明确的宪法法案，但那并不是我们面前的法案。我很抱歉我们没有这样做。

00:28:44.190 --> 00:28:54.690
<v Ira Glass>The governor and secretary of state both turned down a request for interviews. The commission didn't finally vote till after midnight, so they missed that deadline set by the constitution.
<v Ira Glass>州长和州务卿都拒绝了采访请求。委员会直到午夜过后才最终投票，所以他们错过了宪法规定的那个截止日期。

00:28:54.690 --> 00:28:56.820
<v Clerk>Co-Chair Senator Sykes.
<v Clerk>联合主席赛克斯参议员。

00:28:56.820 --> 00:28:57.570
<v Vernon Sykes>No.
<v Vernon Sykes>不。

00:28:57.570 --> 00:28:59.070
<v Clerk>Co-Chair Speaker Cupp.
<v Clerk>联合主席库普议长。

00:28:59.070 --> 00:29:00.000
<v Robert Cupp>Yes.
<v Robert Cupp>是。

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:01.980
<v Clerk>Governor DeWine?
<v Clerk>德温州长？

00:29:01.980 --> 00:29:21.210
<v Ira Glass>Even Republicans who were critical of the maps voted for them. They passed on party lines-- 5 to 2. This is challenged in court, and the Ohio Supreme Court did not buy Huffman's explanation at all about the definition of the word "results." They ruled the map unconstitutional-- back to the drawing board.
<v Ira Glass>甚至连批评地图的共和党人也投票赞成了它们。它们以党派路线通过——5比2。这在法庭上受到挑战，俄亥俄州最高法院根本不买赫夫曼关于“结果”一词定义的解释。他们裁定地图违宪——回到制图板。

00:29:21.210 --> 00:29:40.080
<v Ira Glass>Republicans created a second set of maps, again, on their own, again, with no input from the Democrats on the committee. This time, they tried something different. They gave the Democrats a few more districts that leaned their way but not as many as the Supreme Court called for. But there was a new problem, which the court pointed out when they rejected this set of maps.
<v Ira Glass>共和党人再次制作了第二套地图，又是他们自己，同样没有任何来自委员会中民主党人的投入。这一次，他们尝试了一些不同的东西。他们给了民主党人几个更倾向于他们的选区，但没有最高法院要求的那么多。但出现了一个新问题，法院在否决这套地图时指出了这一点。

00:29:40.080 --> 00:29:57.780
<v Ira Glass>A third of the Democratic seats were just barely Democratic. Like, really, these seats were toss-ups, where Democrats had just a tiny fraction more voters than Republicans, like less than half of 1% more voters in most of the cases. Again, David Pepper used to chair the Democratic Party in the state.
<v Ira Glass>三分之一的民主党席位只是勉强是民主党的。真的，这些席位是五五开，民主党选民只比共和党多一点点，在大多数情况下，比选民多出的比例不到0.5%。还是戴维·佩珀，过去曾担任该州民主党主席。

00:29:57.780 --> 00:30:13.570
<v David Pepper>What the Supreme Court said correctly was, the only seats that were at risk of being lost were Democratic seats. That is not a fair map. You need to have in your side as many competitive seats as Democrats do.
<v David Pepper>最高法院正确指出的是，唯一有失去风险的席位是民主党席位。那不是一张公平的地图。你需要像民主党一样在你的这一侧拥有同样多的竞争性席位。

00:30:13.570 --> 00:30:34.650
<v Ira Glass>The Supreme Court said no to the second map because it violated the part of the constitution that says a map can't, quote, "favor or disfavor one party over another." The court gave the commission 10 days to try again. And the commission didn't meet until the 10th day, the very last day, February 17, 2022. And this time--
<v Ira Glass>最高法院对第二张地图说了不，因为它违反了宪法中规定地图不能（引用）“偏袒或不偏袒一方”的部分。法院给了委员会10天时间再试一次。而委员会直到第10天，也就是最后一天，2022年2月17日才开会。而这一次——

00:30:34.650 --> 00:30:37.380
<v Katy Shanahan>The commission just didn't pass a map.
<v Katy Shanahan>委员会根本没有通过地图。

00:30:37.380 --> 00:30:39.130
<v Ira Glass>Again, Katy Shanahan.
<v Ira Glass>还是凯蒂·沙纳汉。

00:30:39.130 --> 00:31:01.200
<v Katy Shanahan>The Republicans never offered any map. They just didn't do it. And they filed with the court, saying, yeah, sorry, we just reached an impasse. We don't know-- we don't know how to get around it, you know? We don't know how to make you all happy on the court. The Democrats don't want to agree to any of our maps. So I guess we're just not going to have a map.
<v Katy Shanahan>共和党人从来没有提供任何地图。他们只是没有这样做。他们向法院提交文件，说，是的，抱歉，我们只是陷入了僵局。我们不知道——我们不知道怎么绕过它，你知道？我们不知道怎么让你们法院里的所有人满意。民主党人不想同意我们的任何地图。所以我想我们就不会有地图了。

00:31:01.200 --> 00:31:10.350
<v Frank LaRose>The mapmakers, the majority mapmakers, are telling us that they don't believe that we can constitutionally do what the court majority has asked us to do.
<v Frank LaRose>制图者，多数派制图者，正在告诉我们，他们不相信我们能在宪法上做到法院多数派要求我们做的事情。

00:31:10.350 --> 00:31:14.010
<v Ira Glass>This is Republican Secretary of State Frank LaRose at that February hearing.
<v Ira Glass>这是共和党州务卿弗兰克·拉罗斯在二月份的那次听证会上。

00:31:14.010 --> 00:31:30.550
<v Frank LaRose>This is one of those classic cases of what we want versus what we can accomplish. Those who are looking to cast blame and score political points will perhaps represent that the situation we're in is simply because of a lack of will. I don't believe that that's the case.
<v Frank LaRose>这是我们想要的东西与我们可以实现的东西之间经典的案例之一。那些试图归咎责任并获取政治分数的人可能会说，我们所处的境地仅仅是因为缺乏意愿。我不相信那是真的。

00:31:30.550 --> 00:31:46.480
<v Ira Glass>Just to be clear about this, the Republicans are right when they say that it's not so easy to make a map for Ohio that gives Democrats nearly half the districts like the constitution mandates. Because like in many states, the Democrats in Ohio are mostly bunched up in the big cities. In lots of counties, there just aren't so many of them.
<v Ira Glass>为了清楚起见，当共和党人说为俄亥俄州制作一张像宪法强制规定的那样给民主党近一半选区的地图并不容易时，他们是对的。因为像许多州一样，俄亥俄州的民主党人大多聚集在大城市。在很多县，他们的人数并不多。

00:31:46.480 --> 00:32:07.320
<v Ira Glass>So to give them enough districts without breaking any of the other rules, like splitting up counties and townships, it's tricky. Democrats, however, and nonpartisan groups like the League of Women Voters say it isn't that hard a problem. That same day, February 17, the Democrats introduced a map that they made that gave them the number of districts that the Supreme Court said would be constitutionally correct.
<v Ira Glass>所以要在不破坏任何其他规则的情况下给他们足够的选区，比如分裂县和乡镇，这很棘手。然而，民主党人和像“妇女选民联盟”这样的无党派团体说，这不是一个那么困难的问题。同一天，2月17日，民主党人介绍了一张他们制作的地图，给予了他们最高法院所说的宪法上正确的选区数量。

00:32:07.320 --> 00:32:21.660
<v Allison Russo>All other requirements of the constitution are met. No one has shown constitutional violations in these maps, so I urge a yes vote for adoption of the maps that have been proposed in this motion.
