<v Vernon Sykes>是的。

00:42:43.480 --> 00:42:47.020
<v Matt Huffman>Yep. Just a couple responses. First--
<v Matt Huffman>是的。只是几个回应。首先——

00:42:47.020 --> 00:42:49.330
<v Ira Glass>Again, Senator Matt Huffman.
<v Ira Glass>还是马特·赫夫曼参议员。

00:42:49.330 --> 00:43:34.460
<v Matt Huffman>Your statement that these mapmakers have come up with a constitutional map-- well, I don't know that that's true, and I'm not sure how you know it's true because we never saw that. These mapmakers, doing a tremendous job in a very short period of time that was dictated by the court, have not produced a Senate map and are not able to confirm that they have provided a constitutional House map. And what I'm simply saying is we have a deadline today. It may be that Leader Russo knows what the Supreme Court is thinking, but the order says today is the day.
<v Matt Huffman>你关于这些制图师已经拿出一张宪法地图的陈述——嗯，我不知道那是真的，而且我不确定你怎么知道它是真的，因为我们从来没看到那个。这些制图师，在法庭规定的很短时间内做得非常出色，没有制作出参议院地图，也无法确认他们提供了一张宪法众议院地图。而我简单地说的是，我们今天有一个截止日期。可能是鲁索领导知道最高法院在想什么，但命令说今天是那一天。

00:43:34.460 --> 00:43:42.160
<v Ira Glass>In fact, the Supreme Court specifically said in the order, quote, "No requests or stipulations for extension of time shall be filed."
<v Ira Glass>事实上，最高法院在命令中明确表示，引用“不得提交任何延长时间的请求或规定。”

00:43:42.160 --> 00:44:19.980
<v Matt Huffman>And that we have to do it by today. So that, I mean, that's simply what the order is. If somehow, some way, the mapmaker is able to produce a constitutional map that four members of the commission will support, and that is all done by 10:30, then maybe. But it doesn't appear that that's going to happen. So we have to have something that we can provide to the court today, which is what we're ordered to do.
<v Matt Huffman>而且我们必须在今天之前做到。所以那个，我的意思是，那就是命令简单的内容。如果不知何故，无论如何，制图师能够制作出一张将有四名委员会成员支持的宪法地图，并且所有这一切在10:30之前完成，那么也许。但看起来不会发生那种事。所以我们必须有可以今天提供给法院的东西，这就是我们被命令去做的事情。

00:44:19.980 --> 00:44:33.750
<v Vernon Sykes>It seems to be no end to the arrogance of the supermajority. Any other comments?
<v Vernon Sykes>超级多数派的傲慢似乎无止境。还有其他评论吗？

00:44:33.750 --> 00:44:45.990
<v Ira Glass>After that, the commission votes along party lines. They tell staff to make a new version of the old unconstitutional map, though they do allow the independent mapmakers to continue on their independent maps as well. After all, maybe they'll finish on time.
<v Ira Glass>在那之后，委员会按党派路线投票。他们告诉工作人员制作旧违宪地图的新版本，尽管他们也允许独立制图师继续制作他们的独立地图。毕竟，也许他们会准时完成。

00:44:45.990 --> 00:44:49.110
<v Vernon Sykes>The motion is approved and so ordered.
<v Vernon Sykes>动议被批准并如此命令。

00:44:49.110 --> 00:45:05.430
<v Ira Glass>That's very late afternoon, near 6 PM. Then they go into recess. When they reconvene at 9:30 that night, just an hour before the 10:30 deadline Matt Huffman tried to set for them, one of the independent mapmakers they hired, the Republican one, Doug Johnson, gives an update.
<v Ira Glass>那是下午很晚，接近下午6点。然后他们休会。当他们在那天晚上9:30重新开会时，距离马特·赫夫曼试图为他们设定的10:30截止日期仅剩一个小时，他们聘请的独立制图师之一，共和党的那位，道格·约翰逊，给出了一个更新。

00:45:05.430 --> 00:45:30.150
<v Mapmaker Doug>So co-chairs, members of the commission, as you've hopefully seen, about an hour, I guess about two hours ago now, we did finish a full house map and distributed that and have moved on to the Senate map. We're making progress as fast as humanly possible in an effort to get this done this evening.
<v Mapmaker Doug>所以联合主席，委员会成员，正如你们希望已经看到的那样，大约一个小时，我想大约两小时前，我们确实完成了一张完整的众议院地图并分发了它，并且已经转到了参议院地图。为了努力今晚完成这项工作，我们正以人类可能的最快速度取得进展。

00:45:30.150 --> 00:46:11.620
<v Ira Glass>He says that he figures they'll be finished with the Senate map in 45 minutes or an hour. Then he says that they will still need to do some refinements and tweaks to the House map. Commission members have asked for some revisions that haven't been fixed yet. The House map that they made has Republican majorities in 54% of the districts, like the Supreme Court called for. And unlike the previous unconstitutional map, where all the toss-up districts, 19 of them, were Democratic-leaning, in this new map, the independent one, each party has an equal number of toss-up districts-- three Democratic, three Republican, again, like the Supreme Court called for. Matt Huffman and the Republicans are not having it. This is Bob Cupp, the Republican co-chair.
<v Ira Glass>他说他认为他们会在45分钟或一小时内完成参议院地图。然后他说他们仍然需要对众议院地图进行一些改进和调整。委员会成员要求进行一些还没修复的修订。他们制作的众议院地图在54%的选区中有共和党多数，就像最高法院要求的那样。而且与之前的违宪地图不同，那里所有五五开的选区，19个，都是倾向民主党的，在这张新地图，独立的那张中，每个政党都有同样数量的五五开选区——三个民主党，三个共和党，还是像最高法院要求的那样。马特·赫夫曼和共和党人根本不买账。这是鲍勃·库普，共和党联合主席。

00:46:11.620 --> 00:46:12.430
<v Robert Cupp>Mr. Co-Chair.
<v Robert Cupp>联合主席先生。

00:46:12.430 --> 00:46:14.340
<v Vernon Sykes>Yes.
<v Vernon Sykes>是的。

00:46:14.340 --> 00:46:47.290
<v Robert Cupp>I think-- in spite of all of the work that's been done, and I know that the consultants came in and they worked extremely hard, I think it is not feasible to expect that we're going to have that in time to be uploaded to the secretary of state's office in compliance with the court's order. So I would just-- so what I'm going to do is to move, I guess, what has been referred to as the 328 Cupp Plan.
<v Robert Cupp>我想——尽管已经做了所有的工作，而且我知道顾问们进来了，他们工作非常努力，我认为期望我们在时间上来得及将其上传到州务卿办公室以符合法院命令是不现实的。所以我只是——所以我打算做的动议，我猜，被称为328库普计划的东西。

00:46:47.290 --> 00:46:52.780
<v Ira Glass>This is the map that had been found unconstitutional with some small changes that they'd made since 6 PM.
<v Ira Glass>这是那张被发现违宪但自下午6点以来做了些小改动的地图。

00:46:52.780 --> 00:46:56.770
<v Robert Cupp>And move that the commission adopt that plan.
<v Robert Cupp>并提议委员会采用该计划。

00:46:56.770 --> 00:46:58.120
<v Allison Russo>Objection.
<v Allison Russo>反对。

00:46:58.120 --> 00:47:00.160
<v Matt Huffman>Second the motion.
<v Matt Huffman>附议该动议。

00:47:00.160 --> 00:47:02.170
<v Allison Russo>Mr. Co-Chair.
<v Allison Russo>联合主席先生。

00:47:02.170 --> 00:47:03.370
<v Vernon Sykes>Leader Russo.
<v Vernon Sykes>鲁索领导。

00:47:03.370 --> 00:47:10.720
<v Ira Glass>Again, this is Democrat Allison Russo. She's been given a handout with information about this newly revised Republican map.
<v Ira Glass>是的，这是民主党人艾利森·鲁索。她拿到了一份关于这张新修订的共和党地图的信息讲义。

00:47:10.720 --> 00:47:38.170
<v Allison Russo>Thank you, Mr. Co-Chair. I would just like to say that this process is and this motion and this map that's been put before us is a complete farce. I literally have been handed spreadsheets that have population deviations on them, nothing about partisan lean, nothing about symmetry. This is useless information.
<v Allison Russo>谢谢，联合主席先生。我只是想说这个过程以及这个动议和摆在我们面前的这张地图是一个完全的闹剧。我真的被递给了上面有人口偏差的电子表格，没有任何关于党派倾向的信息，没有任何关于对称性的信息。这是无用的信息。

00:47:38.170 --> 00:47:50.530
<v Ira Glass>Bob Cupp tells her that it's a 54 to 46 map, like it had been before. But now, instead of 19 Democratic tossup districts, there were 17. Republicans still have no toss-up districts.
<v Ira Glass>鲍勃·库普告诉她这是一张54比46的地图，就像以前一样。但现在，不是19个民主党五五开选区，而是17个。共和党仍然没有五五开选区。

00:47:50.530 --> 00:47:55.660
<v Allison Russo>And your assessment is that this addresses the symmetry concerns by the Supreme Court?
<v Allison Russo>而你的评估是这解决了最高法院提出的对称性担忧？

00:47:55.660 --> 00:48:00.040
<v Ira Glass>Symmetry concerns-- that each party has a similar number of tossup districts.
<v Ira Glass>对称性担忧——即每个政党都有相似数量的五五开选区。

00:48:00.040 --> 00:48:10.500
<v Robert Cupp>This moves closer to it, and it is the best that can be done in the time that was available under the court's requirement to adopt the plan by March the 28th.
<v Robert Cupp>这更接近它，而且在法院要求在3月28日之前采用计划的时间内，这是能做到的最好的了。

00:48:10.500 --> 00:48:16.420
<v Allison Russo>OK, well, I took a lot of math in college. I would disagree with that.
<v Allison Russo>好的，好吧，我在大学学了很多数学。我不同意这一点。

00:48:16.420 --> 00:48:18.130
<v Allison Russo>[LAUGHTER]
<v Allison Russo>（笑声）

00:48:18.130 --> 00:48:39.280
<v Ira Glass>She asked if they could recess so commissioners could look at the map and if they can make suggestions to change it. Huffman says there's no time for either. Somebody points out that this was Huffman's problem with the independent mapmakers' map, that the commissioners hadn't seen it and that there wouldn't be time to make suggestions to change it. Huffman repeats, the independent map can't be done by midnight.
<v Ira Glass>她问是否可以休会以便委员们可以查看地图，如果他们可以提出修改建议。赫夫曼说无论是哪一个都没有时间。有人指出这是赫夫曼对独立制图师地图的问题，即委员们没有看到它，而且没有时间提出修改建议。赫夫曼重复，独立地图不可能在午夜前完成。

00:48:39.280 --> 00:48:55.570
<v Allison Russo>Mr. Co-Chair, I'm going to make one more motion. I move that we, the commission, directs Eric Clark, who is the commission's attorney with the attorney general's office, to prepare an emergency motion asking the court for an extension of 12 hours.
<v Allison Russo>联合主席先生，我要再做一个动议。我动议我们，委员会，指示埃里克·克拉克，他是委员会与总检察长办公室的律师，准备一份紧急动议，要求法院延期12小时。

00:48:55.570 --> 00:49:08.610
<v Matt Huffman>I object. Mr. Co-Chair, we have a motion on the floor regarding-- I don't even know if Mr. Clark's available, but we've already, several times, read from the Supreme Court's decision, so--
<v Matt Huffman>我反对。联合主席先生，我们有一个关于的动议在桌面上——我甚至不知道克拉克先生是否有空，但我们已经几次宣读了最高法院的决定，所以——

00:49:08.610 --> 00:49:12.190
<v Ira Glass>And with that, they vote on the Republican map.
<v Ira Glass>随之，他们对共和党地图进行了投票。

00:49:12.190 --> 00:49:17.780
<v Clerk>This is a motion to adopt a revised 328 Cupp map.
<v Clerk>这是一个采用修订后的328库普地图的动议。

00:49:17.780 --> 00:49:22.240
<v Audience>No! No. No! No! No! No! No!
<v Audience>不！不。不！不！不！不！不！

00:49:22.240 --> 00:49:22.860
<v Clerk>Governor DeWine?
<v Clerk>德温州长？

00:49:22.860 --> 00:49:27.680
<v Audience>Shame on you. Cheaters. Shame on you. Cheaters! Cheat!
<v Audience>为你们感到羞耻。骗子。为你们感到羞耻。骗子！骗！

00:49:27.680 --> 00:49:32.960
<v Vernon Sykes>Quiet down, please. Please, let's have order. We still have to have order here, please.
<v Vernon Sykes>请安静一下。请，请保持秩序。我们仍然必须在这里保持秩序，请。

00:49:32.960 --> 00:49:42.050
<v Ira Glass>Jen Miller, the executive director of the League of Women Voters, they were the ones who sued to get the Republican maps overturned three times already, was there in the hearing room for this.
<v Ira Glass>“妇女选民联盟”执行主任詹·米勒，他们是起诉推翻共和党地图四次的团体，在听证会现场参加了这次会议。

00:49:42.050 --> 00:50:01.610
<v Jen Miller>Fascinatingly, this Ohio redistricting commission, that had blown through so many deadlines that were in the constitution and deadlines given to them by the Ohio Supreme Court, decides that deadlines matter and that they needed to get a map done, and they pass it along a simple majority.
<v Jen Miller>迷人的是，这个在宪法中耗费了如此多截止日期以及俄亥俄州最高法院给予的截止日期的俄亥俄州选区重划委员会，决定截止日期很重要，并且他们需要完成一张地图，然后他们以简单多数票通过了它。

00:50:01.610 --> 00:50:05.240
<v Ira Glass>What was it like in the room?
<v Ira Glass>房间里是什么样的？

00:50:05.240 --> 00:50:32.980
<v Jen Miller>You know, it was interesting because we all knew that-- [LAUGHS] we knew not to be hopeful, but we were hopeful. And it was just such a letdown. Just disappointed, kind of like when you-- you've got a crush on a guy, [LAUGHS] and you think that he's finally going to do the right thing, and then he still doesn't. And you kind of, in your gut, knew that would probably happen. But you're hopeful. I cried, Ira. I cried.
<v Jen Miller>你知道，这很有趣，因为我们都知道——（笑声）我们知道不要充满希望，但我们确实充满了希望。而这真是一个大失望。只是失望，就像当你——你对一个家伙着迷，（笑声）而你认为他终于要做正确的事情了，然后他仍然没有做。而在你的肠子里，你那种可能预感到那种事会发生。但你充满了希望。我哭了，艾拉。我哭了。

00:50:32.980 --> 00:50:34.180
<v Ira Glass>You cried.
<v Ira Glass>你哭了。

00:50:34.180 --> 00:50:54.640
<v Jen Miller>Yeah. Because I had done nothing. [LAUGHS] I had spent all of my time living and breathing and dreaming maps.
<v Jen Miller>是的。因为我什么都没做。（笑声）我把所有的时间都用来生活、呼吸和梦想地图。

00:50:54.640 --> 00:51:08.420
<v Ira Glass>Allison Russo told me she feels sure the Supreme Court would have granted them an extension because, for the first time, the commission was drafting a map together, in public, exactly like the court had called for. But you know, really, there's no need to speculate about this.
<v Ira Glass>艾利森·鲁索告诉我，她确信最高法院会批准他们的延期，因为这是第一次委员会在一起、在公开场合、完全按照法院要求的那样起草地图。但你知道，真的，不需要对这件事进行猜测。

00:51:08.420 --> 00:51:08.690
<v Maureen O'Connor>Hello?
<v Maureen O'Connor>你好？

00:51:08.690 --> 00:51:11.500
<v Ira Glass>Hello. Is this Justice O'Connor?
<v Ira Glass>你好。请问是奥康纳法官吗？

00:51:11.500 --> 00:51:12.010
<v Maureen O'Connor>Yes.
<v Maureen O'Connor>是的。

00:51:12.010 --> 00:51:13.600
<v Ira Glass>Yes, this is Ira.
<v Ira Glass>是的，我是艾拉。

00:51:13.600 --> 00:51:14.920
<v Maureen O'Connor>Hi, how are you?
<v Maureen O'Connor>你好，你好吗？

00:51:14.920 --> 00:51:35.950
<v Ira Glass>Meet Maureen O'Connor, the Chief Justice of the Ohio Supreme Court, at least through December when she retires. A lifelong Republican and the swing vote that rejected Ohio's election maps as gerrymandered seven times this past year, she knew all about the map that had been made by the independent mapmakers. I told her how the commission never submitted it because they were afraid they wouldn't make their deadline.
<v Ira Glass>会见俄亥俄州最高法院首席大法官莫琳·奥康纳，至少到十二月她退休为止。一位终身共和党人，也是过去一年七次驳回俄亥俄州选区划分不公选举地图的摇摆投票者，她对独立制图师制作的地图了如指掌。我告诉她委员会从来没有提交它，因为他们担心赶不上截止日期。

00:51:35.950 --> 00:52:08.730
<v Maureen O'Connor>OK, that is-- what can I say? If that was their understanding, contacting the court at 9:00 at night, whatever, would have been, can we have 24 more hours? Absolutely. That would have happened. Would we have rejected something that was given to us at 9:00 in the morning? Absolutely not. Absolutely not.
<v Maureen O'Connor>好的，那是——我能说什么呢？如果那是他们的理解，晚上9点联系法院，无论什么，都会是，我们可以多要24小时吗？绝对。那本来会发生的。我们会拒绝在早上9点给我们的东西吗？绝对不会。绝对不会。

00:52:08.730 --> 00:52:43.980
<v Ira Glass>Here's how this played out. Some Republican allies took the case to federal court where a three-judge panel with two Trump appointees told the redistricting commission, if you can't come to an agreement on a map that's constitutional, well, you just use the Republicans' unconstitutional gerrymandered map. After that, unsurprisingly, the commission didn't even try to come to an agreement on a map, never even held a meeting with that purpose. That's why Ohio is going to the polls this week on the gerrymandered maps.
<v Ira Glass>这就是事情的发展。一些共和党盟友将案件带到了联邦法院，在那里，一个有两名特朗普任命者组成的三个法官小组告诉选区重划委员会，如果你不能就一张符合宪法的地图达成一致，嗯，你们就使用共和党违宪的选区划分不公地图。在那之后，不出所料，委员会甚至没有尝试就地图达成一致，从来没有举行过以那个为目的的会议。这就是为什么俄亥俄州本周在选区划分不公的地图上进行投票。

00:52:43.980 --> 00:53:05.130
<v Ira Glass>I got interested in all this, wondering how lawmakers could defy their own constitution over and over. I'm talking about Huffman, the person who drove this process more than anybody else. Here's what I learned. In his telling, Republicans were not defying the Supreme Court. He says they tried to comply, and the court kept telling them no. He thinks the court's rulings misread the constitution.
<v Ira Glass>我开始对所有这些感兴趣，想知道立法者如何能够一次又一次地无视他们自己的宪法。我说的是赫夫曼，那个比任何人都更推动这个过程的人。这就是我学到的。按照他的说法，共和党人并没有无视最高法院。他说他们试图遵守，而法院一直告诉他们不。他认为法院的裁决误读了宪法。

00:53:05.130 --> 00:53:25.530
<v Ira Glass>The way Matt Huffman reads this part of the constitution, which, of course, he had a big hand in writing, he believes that if you follow all the kind of mechanical rules for how to draw maps, keeping districts compact, not dividing counties too many times, that kind of thing, then you actually can ignore the part of the rules that are about proportional fairness, the rules that dictate how many districts go to each party.
<v Ira Glass>马特·赫夫曼阅读宪法这部分的方式，当然，他在书写方面起了很大作用，他相信，如果你遵循所有用于绘制地图的机械规则，保持选区紧凑，不过多划分县，那类事情，那么你实际上可以忽略那些关于比例公平的规则，那些规定每个政党获得多少选区的规则。

00:53:25.530 --> 00:53:34.170
<v Matt Huffman>This section about proportional voting is not required. It's aspirational only.
<v Matt Huffman>关于比例投票的这一节不是必需的。它只是有志向的。

00:53:34.170 --> 00:53:49.590
<v Ira Glass>Of course, the Supreme Court of Ohio sees that very differently. But there is wording in there that Huffman can point to. One of the things that Chief Justice O'Connor told me was that she thought that most Ohioans don't understand what kind of flawed, leaky boat this constitutional amendment is.
<v Ira Glass>当然，俄亥俄州最高法院对此有着非常不同的看法。但其中有赫夫曼可以指出的措辞。奥康纳大法官告诉我的事情之一是，她认为大多数俄亥俄州人并不明白这个宪法修正案是一艘多么有缺陷、漏水的船。

00:53:49.590 --> 00:54:10.530
<v Ira Glass>And I think Huffman really believed from the start that when they wrote this language, he'd created rules that would allow him to get his map through, that would let him get any map through with any mix of Democratic and Republican districts because he believes, again, that the language in the constitution says that if you follow all the other rules in drawing districts, it doesn't matter how many districts each party gets.
<v Ira Glass>而且我认为赫夫曼真的从一开始就相信，当他们写这段语言时，他创造了规则，这将允许他让他的地图通过，这将让他让任何地图通过，带有任何民主党和共和党选区的组合，因为他相信，还是那样，宪法中的语言说，如果你在绘制选区时遵循所有其他规则，每个政党获得多少选区并不重要。

00:54:10.530 --> 00:54:31.460
<v Ira Glass>In fact, we know from court documents that he instructed the Republican mapmakers to get everything else about the maps correct. Be sure to follow all those other rules, and don't worry about how many districts you give to the Democrats. But beyond that, in a bigger way, Huffman told me he thinks that there are real limits to what courts can tell legislators to do, just in general.
<v Ira Glass>事实上，我们从法庭文件中知道，他指示共和党制图师纠正地图的其他所有内容。一定要遵循所有那些其他规则，不要担心你给民主党多少选区。但除此之外，以一种更大的方式，赫夫曼告诉我，他认为法院可以告诉立法者做什么，只是总体而言，存在真正的限制。

00:54:31.460 --> 00:55:02.770
<v Ira Glass>And not long after Huffman and I talked, Republicans in Ohio took one of their redistricting cases to the US Supreme Court. Basically, it's like a North Carolina case you may have heard of that the court is going to hear this year, where one side is arguing that the state supreme courts have no power at all over any state legislatures. Fifty state chief justices, Republicans and Democrats, filed a brief with the Supreme Court arguing against that point of view.
<v Ira Glass>而在赫夫曼和我交谈后不久，俄亥俄州的共和党人将他们的一项选区重划案件带到了美国最高法院。基本上，这就像你可能听说过的北卡罗来纳州案件，法院今年要听审，其中一方辩称州最高法院对任何州立法机关根本没有任何权力。五十位州首席大法官，共和党人和民主党人，向最高法院提交了一份反对那种观点的简报。

00:55:02.770 --> 00:55:23.600
<v Ira Glass>OK, so what happens next? Well, even while they're arguing that state supreme court justices should have less power, Republicans in Ohio right now are fighting to get different justices onto the state supreme court, justices who might agree with Matt Huffman's arguments and approve Republican maps that the current court rejects as unconstitutional. Three seats on the court are open.
<v Ira Glass>好的，接下来会发生什么？嗯，即使在他们辩称州最高法院大法官应该拥有更少权力的同时，俄亥俄州的共和党人现在正在为让不同的法官进入州最高法院而奋斗，法官们可能同意马特·赫夫曼的论点，并批准法院驳回为违宪的共和党地图。法院有三个席位空缺。

00:55:23.600 --> 00:55:44.780
<v Ira Glass>Every time the court rejected the Republican maps, it was by the narrowest margin, a four to three vote. The two parties have spent over $10 million on these Supreme Court races so far, the Republicans outspending the Democrats nearly two to one. The campaigns are unashamedly partisan. Here's one of the Republican supreme court candidates speaking at an event at a boathouse about the three judicial candidates.
<v Ira Glass>每次法院驳回共和党地图时，都是以最窄的幅度，四比三的投票。两个党派在这些最高法院竞选中已经花费了超过1000万美元，共和党人的支出几乎是民主党人的两倍。竞选活动毫无羞耻地具有党派性。这是一位共和党最高法院候选人在船屋举行的活动中谈到三名司法候选人时的讲话。

00:55:44.780 --> 00:55:51.920
<v Patrick Fischer>If you vote for three and all three win, we take back conservative control of the court, if that's important to you.
<v Patrick Fischer>如果你投票给三个人，且三个人都赢了，我们就收回法院的保守派控制权，如果这对你很重要的话。

00:55:51.920 --> 00:55:53.780
<v Patrick Fischer>[APPLAUSE]
<v Patrick Fischer>（掌声）

00:55:53.780 --> 00:56:00.920
<v Patrick Fischer>And just for myself, don't forget, that's Fischer with a C for the constitution and common sense. Thanks.
<v Patrick Fischer>就我而言，别忘了，那是带着代表宪法和常识的C的费舍尔。谢谢。

00:56:00.920 --> 00:56:17.330
<v Ira Glass>If electing justices who will side with Matt Huffman's reading of the constitution is the Republican hope right now, the Democrats and good government groups have drawn other conclusions from their exhausting year-long slog at the redistricting commission. Here's Jen Miller of the League of Women Voters.
<v Ira Glass>如果选举会站在马特·赫夫曼宪法阅读立场上的法官是共和党人现在的希望，民主党人和良政团体已经从他们选区重划委员会长达一年的令人精疲力竭的艰苦工作中得出了其他结论。这是“妇女选民联盟”的詹·米勒。

00:56:17.330 --> 00:56:25.950
<v Jen Miller>The lesson is that politicians are going to be politicians, and we have to take them out of the equation completely.
<v Jen Miller>教训是政客终究会是政客，我们必须把他们完全剔除出这个等式。

00:56:25.950 --> 00:56:27.590
<v Ira Glass>How?
<v Ira Glass>怎么做？

00:56:27.590 --> 00:56:48.140
<v Jen Miller>I don't-- we're going to have to have another ballot initiative. It's going to be hard. It's going to be expensive. It's going to take a long time. But if you look around the states and see which places redistricting went well, they were independent, balanced commissions.
<v Jen Miller>我不知道——我们将不得不进行另一次投票倡议。这会很困难。这会很昂贵。这需要很长时间。但如果你环顾各州，看看哪些地方选区重划做得好，它们都是独立的、平衡的委员会。

00:56:48.140 --> 00:56:54.150
<v Ira Glass>Independent in this case means it's not politicians or public officials drawing the maps.
<v Ira Glass>在这种情况下，独立意味着不是政客或公职人员绘制地图。

00:56:54.150 --> 00:56:54.840
<v Ira Glass>If you want to imagine another way that things could've gone in Ohio this year, you can just look at New York State. In New York, it was the Democrats who run the state who drew a heavily gerrymandered map. They gave themselves majorities in 22 of the state's 26 congressional districts that determine who goes to the US House of Representatives. This year New York, like Ohio, had a brand new constitutional amendment to stop gerrymandering. And this year, the New York State Supreme Court struck down the Democrat's gerrymandered map as illegal, unconstitutional.
<v Ira Glass>如果你想想象在俄亥俄州今年可能发生的另一种方式，你可以看看纽约州。在纽约，是管理该州的民主党人绘制了一张严重选区划分不公的地图。他们在该州26个国会选区中的22个中给了自己多数优势，这些选区决定了谁去美国众议院。今年纽约，像俄亥俄州一样，有了一个全新的宪法修正案来停止选区划分不公。而今年，纽约州最高法院以非法、违宪为由驳回了民主党人选区划分不公的地图。

00:56:54.840 --> 00:56:55.380
<v Ira Glass>A special master appointed by the court redrew the map. The number of safe Democratic districts dropped from 22 to just 15. And  the Democrats stopped fighting. They accepted the court's ruling. That's the map the state will go to the polls with this week.  It'll be that much harder, seven seats harder, for Democrats to keep their control of the US House of Representatives with this un-gerrymandered map.
<v Ira Glass>法院任命的一位特别主管重新绘制了地图。稳固的民主党选区数量从22个下降到只有15个。而民主党人停止了战斗。他们接受了法院的裁决。那是该州本周去投票时将带着的地图。对于民主党人来说，带着这张没有选区划分不公的地图来保持对美国众议院的控制将困难得多，七个席位更难。

00:56:55.380 --> 00:56:55.590
<v Ira Glass>And why did the Democrats in New York accept the court's ruling?  Well for starters, it would've been a lot harder for the Democrats to keep fighting for their illegal map because New York's constitution lets the Supreme Court appoint a special master who works for the court  to draw a new map, and then the court can impose that new map on the entire state.
<v Ira Glass>为什么纽约的民主党人接受了法院的裁决？嗯，首先，民主党人要为他们违法的地图继续战斗会困难得多，因为纽约的宪法允许最高法院任命一位为法院工作的特别主管来绘制一张新地图，然后法院可以强加那张新地图到整个州。

00:56:55.590 --> 00:56:55.860
<v Ira Glass>Ohio's constitution doesn't have anything like that. In fact, the wording in the constitution specifically prohibits it, thanks to Matt Huffman and the Republicans. If a map is bad, if it gerrymanders, the constitution throws the job of redrawing back to politicians on the redistricting commission, who can basically stonewall. And so Republicans in Ohio never redrew their map to give the court what it asked for.
<v Ira Glass>俄亥俄州的宪法没有任何类似的东西。事实上，宪法中的措辞明确禁止它，这要感谢马特·赫夫曼和共和党人。如果一张地图很糟糕，如果它存在选区划分不公，宪法就把重划的工作抛回给选区重划委员会的政客，他们基本上可以拖延。所以俄亥俄州的共和党人从来没有重划他们的地图来给法院它所要求的。

00:56:55.860 --> 00:57:28.740
<v Ira Glass>And they hope they'll never have to, when they get a new set of justices on the court this next year.
<v Ira Glass>他们希望他们永远不必那样做，当他们在明年得到法院的一组新法官时。

00:57:28.740 --> 00:57:49.620
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "I'LL DO IT ANYWAY" BY THE LEMONHEADS]
